MongoDB Array Operator:

MongoDB provides array operators for two main purposes:
a) Querying arrays → Find documents based on array contents.
b) Updating arrays → Add, remove, or control array elements.

Querying arrays

1. $in vs $all vs $size vs $elemMatch

Before learning the update operators, understand these four query operators.

a) $in — Match ANY value

$in checks whether an array contains at least one of the specified values.

Example
db.employees.find({
    skills: {
        $in: ["react", "python"]
    }
});

Meaning:

Find employees whose skills contains react OR python.

If the document is:

{
    skills: ["react", "javascript", "node"]
}

✅ Matches because react exists.
-----------------------------
b) $all — Match ALL values

$all checks whether an array contains all specified values.

db.employees.find({
    skills: {
        $all: ["react", "javascript"]
    }
});

Meaning:

skills must contain react AND javascript.

Example:

{
    skills: ["react", "javascript", "node"]
}

✅ Matches.

But:

{
    skills: ["react", "node"]
}

❌ Doesn't match because javascript is missing.

c)  $in vs $all
$in
→ ANY value
→ OR

$all
→ ALL values
→ AND
Operator	Requirement
$in: ["react","python"]	        React OR Python
$all: ["react","python"]	React AND Python


-------------------
d). $size — Exact Array Length

$size finds arrays containing exactly the specified number of elements.

db.employees.find({
    skills: {
        $size: 3
    }
});

Meaning:

Find employees whose skills array has exactly 3 elements.

Example:

skills: ["react", "node", "mongodb"]

✅ Matches.

skills: ["react", "node"]

❌ Doesn't match.

Important

$size: 3 means:

Exactly 3

NOT:

3 or more

NOT:

at least 3
---------------------
e) $elemMatch — Match One Array Element Against Multiple Conditions

$elemMatch is especially useful when an array contains objects.

Example:

{
    name: "Ali",
    projects: [
        {
            name: "CRM",
            status: "completed"
        },
        {
            name: "Chat App",
            status: "pending"
        }
    ]
}

Find an employee who has a project where:

name = CRM
AND
status = completed

Use:

db.employees.find({
    projects: {
        $elemMatch: {
            name: "CRM",
            status: "completed"
        }
    }
});

MongoDB looks for one project object satisfying both conditions.

Why is this important?

Consider:

projects: [
    {
        name: "CRM",
        status: "pending"
    },
    {
        name: "Chat App",
        status: "completed"
    }
]

There is:

a CRM project ✅
a completed project ✅

But they are different objects.

$elemMatch ensures that both conditions apply to the same array element.

Remember
$elemMatch
→ One array element
→ Multiple conditions
→ Conditions must apply to that same element
